Boutique hotel or staying with a host? Our guests ask us this often, and the answer is not only a matter of taste: the two formats have neither the same status nor the same rules. We run a charming 3-star hotel with 10 rooms near Amboise, and we could not be a French chambre d'hôtes (B&B) even if we wanted to, as the law caps that activity at five rooms. Here are the six differences that really matter when you book.

## Boutique hotel and host-run house: the definitions

A chambre d'hôtes is a furnished room let in the host's own home, where the host welcomes guests in person, within a limit of five rooms and fifteen guests, breakfast included. A boutique hotel is a small hotel with character, with no cap on the number of rooms, which can be rated with stars.

The B&B has a precise definition in the French Tourism Code, articles L324-3 and D324-13. It must be declared at the town hall, the host must live there and welcome guests in person, and each night includes at least bed linen and breakfast.

The boutique hotel, for its part, does not exist in law. It is a hotel, with a hotel's obligations, and the word "boutique" describes a promise: an old building, decoration that is not a chain's, owners who are present. That is why you should look at everything else before trusting the word.

## 1. Size: five rooms at most

The first difference is size: a B&B cannot exceed five rooms or fifteen guests, while a boutique hotel has no cap. Most stay small by choice, between five and twenty rooms.

In practice, this changes the atmosphere. In a three-room guesthouse you meet every other guest at breakfast. In a ten-room boutique hotel like ours, you still get the calm of a home, but a group of twenty can also take the whole place for a birthday.

## 2. Rating: stars or a label

A hotel can be rated from 1 to 5 stars by Atout France (the French tourism development agency), for five years, after a visit by an accredited body. A chambre d'hôtes has no stars: it can join a private label such as Gîtes de France or Clévacances, which has its own criteria.

Stars measure facilities and services, not charm. The scheme checks, for example, the welcome, the size of the rooms or the languages spoken. A B&B label judges rather the comfort of the house and the quality of the welcome. Both say true things, but not the same ones.

![The château's entrance hall, its wooden staircase and a grand piano set beneath the flight of steps](/images/inline/salons-interieurs/salons-interieurs-05.webp "The entrance hall. A boutique hotel is first of all a house that has kept its personality.")

## 3. Breakfast: included or separate

In a chambre d'hôtes, breakfast is always included in the price of the night. In a hotel it is often offered separately, so you can skip it if you prefer a coffee in town.

With us, the homemade breakfast is 15 € per person, served on the terrace when the weather allows. We prefer to show it separately: so always compare two prices with breakfast on an equal footing, otherwise a B&B looks more expensive than it is.

![Breakfast laid on the terrace, croissants in a basket, flowered porcelain and a silver pot in low sunlight](/images/inline/restauration/restauration-10.webp "Breakfast on the terrace, served separately, at 15 € per person.")

## 4. The welcome: in someone's home or with the owners

In a chambre d'hôtes, you sleep at someone's home: the host lives on site and receives you in their house. In a boutique hotel, you sleep in an establishment, but one often run by its owners, who welcome their guests themselves.

This is where the line becomes blurred, and we like that. We live in the château, we know the cellars and the tables of the valley, and we share our addresses as a host would. This is what we call [the spirit of a B&B, within a boutique hotel near Amboise](/en/blog/bed-and-breakfast-amboise): conversation and advice, with the independence of a hotel.

## 5. Service: what is included

A hotel provides continuous service: reception, daily housekeeping, more flexible arrival times, and the safety obligations that apply to establishments receiving the public. In a chambre d'hôtes, service depends on the host, and you usually arrange your arrival time with them.

A boutique hotel is not an ordinary hotel, though: it does not always offer room service or a spa, and ours has neither. What we do have is an enclosed park of 14 hectares, a lap pool heated from April to October and air-conditioned rooms, each one different.

## 6. The table: table d'hôtes or restaurant

The table d'hôtes is a meal shared with the host, a single menu served at the family table, reserved for guests staying in the rooms. A hotel may have a restaurant open to everyone, a table reserved for its residents, or nothing at all.

At the château, the table welcomes residents from 1 May to 30 September, and on Thursdays dinner is Italian; out of season, it opens by reservation for six people or more. I describe what I serve there in our article on the [table d'hôtes in Touraine](/en/blog/table-dhotes-loire-chateau), and the opening times are on the [dining](/en/chateau-dining-touraine) page.

![Climbing rose in flower against a château wall, pines and tall trees of the park under a blue sky](/images/inline/jardin-fleurs/jardin-fleurs-01.webp "A château wall in June. The countryside around is what the two formats often share.")

## Which to choose for a stay in the Loire Valley?

Choose a B&B to live in someone's home and share their table, a boutique hotel to keep that connection with more independence, service and space. For a group of more than fifteen people, only a hotel or a rental can house you all together.

For a couple coming for two nights, the two are equal; it is the atmosphere that decides. For a family with children, the pool and the space of an estate often matter more. Our guide to [where to stay in Amboise](/en/blog/where-to-stay-amboise) also compares the neighbourhoods, and our article on [sleeping in a Loire château](/en/blog/sleep-in-a-loire-chateau) reviews the château options.

Château de la Huberdière is a charming 3-star hotel in Nazelles-Négron, ten minutes from Amboise and 2 hrs 20 from Paris. Discover our 10 rooms and [book your stay at the château](/en/boutique-hotel-amboise): we answer every enquiry within 24 hours.
